## 🌍 3. WAN – Wide Area Network

**Definition**:  
WAN connects LANs and MANs across **different cities, countries, or continents** using long-distance infrastructure.

**Devices and Technologies Used**:
- Core routers
- Undersea fiber optic cables
- Satellites
- VPN/MPLS
- Internet Exchange Points (IXPs)

**Real-World Example**:
> You send a file from Pune to someone in London:
- Your phone connects to Jio → Pune MAN
- Data travels through Jio's national WAN backbone
- Sent across undersea fiber cable to the UK
- UK ISP receives and sends it to your friend's device

✅ This is WAN — a global network connecting multiple MANs and LANs

---

## 🔁 Summary Table

| Type | Full Form | Area Covered | Devices Used | Examples |
|------|-----------|--------------|---------------|----------|
| PAN | Personal Area Network | 1 person / 10m | Bluetooth, USB | Phone + Smartwatch |
| LAN | Local Area Network | Room/Building | Router, Switch | Home Wi-Fi, College Lab |
| MAN | Metropolitan Area Network | City/Wide Campus | Fiber, Routers | Jio/Airtel city network |
| WAN | Wide Area Network | Country/World | Undersea Cables, Core Routers | Internet, Pune → London |

---

## 📌 Final Thoughts

- LAN → local access
- MAN → fast city-level data movement
- WAN → cross-country/global reach
- All are connected in layers, forming the backbone of the internet.
- A SOC analyst must understand how attackers may move from LAN → MAN → WAN and how to track logs across them.

